WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.660
How do we compare growth

2
00:00:01.660 --> 00:00:03.760
rates? We will simplify a

3
00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:06.220
collection of functions, put equivalent

4
00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:08.360
growth rates together, and connect

5
00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:09.860
logarithms to a loop that

6
00:00:09.860 --> 00:00:11.520
repeatedly divides by two.

7
00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:13.960
Begin with a constant function

8
00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:16.180
such as one thousand. Its

9
00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:18.040
value can be large, but

10
00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:19.340
it does not increase with

11
00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:21.460
input size, so its growth

12
00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:22.760
class is constant.

13
00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:26.120
Next put logarithmic functions together,

14
00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:27.820
regardless of their fixed base.

15
00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:30.660
The change-of-base formula turns one

16
00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:32.640
logarithm into a fixed constant

17
00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:34.560
times another, so their Theta

18
00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:35.580
class is the same.

19
00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:37.920
The logarithm of n squared

20
00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:39.600
belongs in that same group,

21
00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:41.580
because it equals twice the

22
00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:43.460
logarithm of n. Do not

23
00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:45.680
confuse squaring inside the logarithm

24
00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:47.560
with squaring the logarithm itself.

25
00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:50.820
Now compare logarithmic growth with

26
00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:51.920
the square root of n

27
00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:54.200
and with n itself. Every

28
00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:55.740
fixed positive power of n

29
00:00:55.740 --> 00:00:58.360
eventually outgrows a logarithm, even

30
00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:00.000
if a small numerical example

31
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:01.660
initially makes the difference look

32
00:01:01.660 --> 00:01:02.000
modest.

33
00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:04.500
Multiplying n by log n

34
00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:06.260
places it above linear growth

35
00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:08.760
but below a quadratic. Larger

36
00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:11.140
fixed polynomial exponents follow, giving

37
00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:12.900
n squared before n to

38
00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:13.300
the fifth.

39
00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:17.200
Fixed-base exponentials eventually outgrow every

40
00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:20.560
fixed-degree polynomial. Changing an exponential

41
00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:22.960
base generally matters: two to

42
00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:24.300
the n, three to the

43
00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:25.880
n, and four to the

44
00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:27.600
n form different growth rates.

45
00:01:28.060 --> 00:01:29.140
Two to the power two

46
00:01:29.140 --> 00:01:31.120
n simplifies to four to

47
00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:32.540
the n. It is not

48
00:01:32.540 --> 00:01:34.300
the double exponential two raised

49
00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:35.400
to two to the n,

50
00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:37.280
so read the parentheses before

51
00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:38.760
rearranging your function cards.

52
00:01:39.220 --> 00:01:42.240
Factorial eventually exceeds any fixed-base

53
00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:44.200
exponential, while n to the

54
00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:46.760
n grows faster still. Our

55
00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:48.580
full ordering therefore runs from

56
00:01:48.580 --> 00:01:51.420
constants through logarithms, powers and

57
00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:54.620
exponentials, then factorial, then n

58
00:01:54.620 --> 00:01:55.320
to the n.

59
00:01:55.820 --> 00:01:57.220
Let us now derive a

60
00:01:57.220 --> 00:01:59.140
logarithm instead of merely recognising

61
00:01:59.140 --> 00:02:01.580
its name. Starting from sixty-four,

62
00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:03.580
repeatedly divide by two to

63
00:02:03.580 --> 00:02:05.560
get thirty-two, sixteen, eight, four,

64
00:02:05.740 --> 00:02:07.520
two, then one, using six

65
00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:07.920
divisions.

66
00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:10.520
For an exact power of

67
00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:12.760
two, solving n divided by

68
00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:13.820
two to the x equals

69
00:02:13.820 --> 00:02:16.220
one gives x equals log

70
00:02:16.220 --> 00:02:18.320
base two of n. Starting

71
00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:20.080
at one and repeatedly doubling

72
00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:21.800
until reaching at least n

73
00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:23.680
instead requires the ceiling of

74
00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:24.400
that logarithm.

75
00:02:24.780 --> 00:02:26.680
For other input sizes, floor

76
00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:28.380
division and the stopping condition

77
00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:30.500
determine the exact integer count.

78
00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:32.260
A loop that keeps dividing

79
00:02:32.260 --> 00:02:33.880
while its value exceeds one

80
00:02:33.880 --> 00:02:35.360
differs in detail from a

81
00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:37.200
real-number loop that stops once

82
00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:39.200
it falls below one, although

83
00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:40.700
both have logarithmic order.

84
00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:43.140
Things to take note of:

85
00:02:43.540 --> 00:02:45.960
simplify first, group Theta ties,

86
00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:48.280
and distinguish logarithm bases from

87
00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:51.180
exponential bases. Derive loop counts

88
00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:52.700
from their updates and stopping

89
00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:54.540
conditions, and remember that a

90
00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:56.740
finite graph illustrates growth but

91
00:02:56.740 --> 00:02:58.280
does not prove the eventual

92
00:02:58.280 --> 00:02:59.700
order, how charming.
